Yes, I have handled OOMKilled issues in production.

Initially, I focused on stabilizing the application by increasing the memory limits and scaling the number of pods so that the load was distributed. This helped restore the service quickly.

After that, I analyzed the memory usage using monitoring tools and observed that memory consumption was gradually increasing over time instead of sudden spikes. This indicated a possible memory leak.

I worked with the development team and identified that the issue was due to unclosed database connections, which were continuously consuming memory. Once the code was fixed, we redeployed the application with optimized resource configurations.

To prevent similar issues in the future, we defined proper resource requests and limits, enabled HPA based on memory usage, and set up alerts and dashboards for better monitoring.

In another case, the issue was due to a sudden traffic spike, which we handled by scaling the application horizontally and tuning autoscaling policies.




